Sure! Here's a simple JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].match(/^(an|a|the)\s+([a-z]+)$/)) {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  if (adjectives.length > 1) {
    const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();
    const newSentence = reversedAdjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.filter(word => !word.match(/^(an|a|the)\s+([a-z]+)$/)).join(" ");
    return newSentence;
  } else {
    return sentence;
  }
}

const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const newSentence = switchAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(newSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function splits the input sentence into an array of words, and then filters out all words that are not adjectives (using a regular expression). It then reverses the order of the adjectives and joins them back into a sentence, along with the remaining words from the original sentence. If there are no adjectives in the sentence, the function returns the original sentence unchanged.

