Here's a simple C function that converts an array of command-line arguments (`argv`) to a null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

char *argv_to_string(int argc, char **argv) {
    int i;
    char *str = malloc(argc * sizeof(char));

    if (!str) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error allocating memory\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(str, argv[i]);
        strcat(str, " ");
    }

    str[strlen(str) - 1] = '\0'; // Remove the last space character

    return str;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *str = argv_to_string(argc, argv);
    printf("Stringified argv: %s\n", str);
    free(str);

    return 0;
}
```

This function takes two arguments: `argc`, which is the number of command-line arguments, and `argv`, which is an array of pointers to the command-line arguments. It allocates memory for a string that can hold all the arguments concatenated together with a space separator, and then iterates over the `argv` array, appending each argument to the string. Finally, it removes the last space character and returns the string.

In the `main` function, we call the `argv_to_string` function and print the resulting string. We also free the memory allocated for the string to avoid memory leaks.

