To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple distance check between each planet before adding it to the array. If the distance between two planets is less than the desired minimum distance, you can generate a new random position for that planet until it meets the criteria. Here's an updated version of the `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        const distance = Math.sqrt(
          (coord - planet.position[index]) ** 2
        );
        return distance < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to call this function with the desired number of planets and minimum distance as arguments:

```javascript
setPlanets(generateRandomPlanets(5, 35));
```

To create a React utility that removes widows, you can use the `useEffect` hook and the `getComputedStyle` method to check if any elements have a width of less than 100%. If an element is found with a width less than 100%, you can adjust its width to prevent it from becoming a widow. Here's an example:

```javascript
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = () => {
  useEffect(() => {
    const handleResize = () => {
      const elements = document.querySelectorAll('[data-remove-widows]');
      elements.forEach((element) => {
        const style = getComputedStyle(element);
        const width = parseFloat(style.width);

        if (width < 100) {
          element.style.width = '100%';
        }
      });
    };

    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div data-remove-widows>
      {/* Your content here */}
    </div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

In this example, we use the `useEffect` hook to listen for the `resize` event. When the event is triggered, we select all elements with the `data-remove-widows` attribute and check their width. If the width is less than 100%, we set the width to 100% to prevent it from becoming a widow.

You can use this component in your React application by importing it and wrapping your content with it:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import RemoveWidows from './RemoveWidows';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <RemoveWidows>
      {/* Your content here */}
    </RemoveWidows>
  );
};

export default App;
```

This will ensure that any elements with a width less than 100% are adjusted to prevent them from becoming widows.

